  "query_preview": "..A trough currently over northern California will dive \nsouthward into southern Nevada tonight and Tuesday, before lifting \nnortheast into Utah Tuesday evening. This system has developed a \nmodest sub-tropical tap and dew points in southern Arizona are \nalready in the upper 40s to lower 50s. This is more than enough to \ngenerate upslope showers tonight and Tuesday, along with frontal \ndynamics over western and northwestern Arizona.\n\nThe problem is that when the upper low shears into Utah, the front \nundergoes frontolysis over the Kaibab Plateau and we do not see as \nmuch precipitation over central and eastern Arizona. What we will \nsee is a bunch of wind, especially north of the Mogollon Rim and a \nWind Advisory has been issued for much of Tuesday. As far as QPF, we \nexpect anywhere from a tenth of an inch to an inch, higher amounts \nover the northwestern zones and lowest along the New Mexico Border. \nSnow levels will fall to around 6500 feet toward the end of the \nevent, but moisture profiles are fairly shallow at this point and \nand any meaningful accumulations will be limited to  areas above \nabout 8000 feet.\n\nDrier westerlies take over for Wednesday and Thursday, before \nanother fast moving front brings chances back to the northern zones \non Friday. This system does not look very impressive or wet at this \ntime, but we will keep an eye on it.",
